Yes! And I think you’ve put your finger on something fundamental about evolution.

Behavior doesn’t merely result from evolution. Behavior can change the conditions under which evolution occurs.

And then evolution changes what behaviors become possible.

It’s a feedback loop, not necessarily a one-way street.

Evolutionary biology actually has several concepts that capture parts of your idea: the Baldwin effect, niche construction, behavioral drive, and phenotypic plasticity.

What I especially like about your mustelid example is that you’re essentially looking at different expressions of a shared ancestral toolkit.

Otter — aquatic specialization

A mustelid lineage adapted to swimming, diving, and hunting in water.

Marten — arboreal specialization

A mustelid equipped for climbing, balancing, and navigating complex forest habitats.

Both are mustelids. But the physical demands of their environments favor different proportions, movement capabilities, and anatomical specializations. Comparative research on musteloids even finds that some aquatic and tree-dwelling species independently developed similar forelimb features useful for rotating the forearm. PubMed +1

Here’s the possibility you’re exploring:

An ancestral animal begins exploiting a different habitat using abilities it already possesses. That changes how it obtains food, escapes predators, competes, and reproduces. Across generations, heritable traits that make those activities more successful may become more common.

Eventually, anatomy can become increasingly specialized around a behavior that once required considerable improvisation.

The behavior helps open the niche. The niche changes the selective pressures. Evolution reshapes the animal. The reshaped animal can explore new behaviors.

And crocodiles make this even more interesting.

Some living crocodiles can actually gallop or bound, lifting their bodies and moving with surprising agility. A 2019 locomotion study documented asymmetrical running gaits across several crocodile species. PMC +1

But here’s a twist: early crocodile relatives already had relatively erect limbs and were predominantly terrestrial. Modern crocodilians subsequently evolved more sprawling, semi-aquatic specializations while retaining some elevated walking capabilities. PubMed Central (PMC) +1

So it isn’t necessarily that crocodiles started chasing prey and then evolved upright legs. Some of that capability may be ancestral.

And that makes your larger idea richer: evolution can preserve, repurpose, diminish, and occasionally reemphasize existing capabilities.

How behavior can feed into evolution

Variation in behavior and anatomy

Organisms explore different opportunities

Different selective pressures emerge

Heritable adaptations accumulate across generations

New capabilities open new behavioral possibilities

The Baldwin effect examines how learning and behavioral flexibility can alter natural selection. Niche construction concerns how organisms modify their own selective environments. Neither requires assuming that acquired bodily changes are automatically inherited. PMC +1

And importantly, behavioral flexibility can sometimes reduce evolutionary pressure for anatomical specialization, because learning already solves the problem. The feedback can accelerate, redirect, or slow evolutionary change.

That brings us right back to something we’ve been exploring across Pantheon.

Play can be a form of evolutionary exploration.

An organism experiments with what its existing body can do. An advantageous behavior opens up a possibility. Over generations, that possibility can influence which heritable traits are favored.

The structure enables the behavior; the behavior changes the environment; the environment changes the evolutionary prospects of the structure.

And there’s a particularly intriguing extension: in humans, culture and technology can change behavioral possibilities much faster than biological evolution. We can alter the selective environment without waiting for our bodies to evolve.

So maybe the deeper principle is:

The player changes the game. The game changes the player. And the next player inherits a different game.

That’s true in a literal evolutionary sense when inherited biological variation and selection are involved, and it’s a useful analogy for learning, culture, and artificial systems through other mechanisms.

Which raises a fascinating question: how many capabilities throughout evolutionary history began not as a perfectly optimized adaptation, but as an organism doing something unusual with what it already had?